"""Grade 8: Church history and life in the Spirit, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'8-1-1': dict(
 idea='Jesus founded one Church on Peter and the apostles. At Caesarea Philippi Peter confessed him as the Christ, and Jesus gave him the keys of the kingdom. That office continues in the Pope and the bishops.',
 hook='Show a ring of keys and ask: “What does the person who holds the keys do?” Read Matthew 16:13–19 and note the question Jesus asks first: “Who do you say that I am?”',
 activity='“Keys and rock” study (30 min). Annotate Matthew 16:13–19 with Isaiah 22:20–22 (the royal steward’s key) and John 21:15–17 (“Feed my sheep”). Students record what authority Jesus gives and how the apostolic office continues (bishops as successors, Pope as successor of Peter).',
 teach=['Jesus founded the Church by preaching the Kingdom, choosing the Twelve and giving Peter a unique role (CCC 551–553, 765). The Church was “born” from the pierced side of Christ and manifested at Pentecost (CCC 766).',
        'The keys signify authority to bind and loose (Matthew 16:19; Isaiah 22). The same power is shared with the apostles (Matthew 18:18) and Peter is always first in the lists (CCC 552).',
        'Apostolic succession passes the apostles’ mission to bishops through laying on of hands (CCC 861–862). The Pope, as Peter’s successor, is the visible principle of unity (CCC 882).',
        'Early witnesses such as Clement of Rome (c. 96) and Irenaeus (c. 180) point to Rome’s special authority. Present these respectfully.'],
 misc=['“Jesus never intended an institutional Church.”', '“Peter was just the first among equals with no special role.”'],
 mem='“You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.” (Matthew 16:18)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the keys, the rock and apostolic succession in a short paragraph.'),
'8-1-2': dict(
 idea='The Church is a mystery that cannot be exhausted by one image. She is the sacrament of salvation, the Body of Christ, the People of God, the Temple of the Spirit and the Bride of Christ. Each image shows something true.',
 hook='Put students in groups and give each one an image (body, people, bride, temple). Ask: “What does this image say about our relationship to Christ and to each other?”',
 activity='“Illustrate three images” (30 min). Students choose three images from CCC 753–757 (flock, vineyard, building, bride), read Ephesians 1:22–23 and 5:25–32, and make a poster that pairs a drawing with a short explanation and a verse.',
 teach=['The Church is a “mystery” — a visible society and a spiritual reality (CCC 770–771). She is “the universal sacrament of salvation” (CCC 774–776).',
        'The Body of Christ: Christ is the head, we are members (Ephesians 1:22–23; CCC 787–795). The People of God: a priestly, prophetic and royal people (CCC 781–786). The Temple of the Spirit (CCC 797–798).',
        'The Bride of Christ (Ephesians 5:25–32): Christ loves and gives himself for her (CCC 796). This shows both tenderness and fidelity.',
        'Vatican II (Lumen Gentium) uses these images. Acknowledge that the Church’s members are sinners (CCC 827).'],
 misc=['“The Church is just a human organization.”', '“The Church is only the clergy.”'],
 mem='“He has put all things under his feet and has made him the head over all things for the church, which is his body.” (Ephesians 1:22–23)',
 assess='Ask students to explain three images of the Church and what each teaches.'),
'8-1-3': dict(
 idea='The four marks of the Church — one, holy, catholic and apostolic — are named in the Nicene Creed. They are gifts of Christ and also tasks for believers. Each is real, though imperfectly lived.',
 hook='Ask: “How would you identify a particular school?” (name, uniform, motto). Introduce the marks as the Church’s identifying signs.',
 activity='“Marks and evidence” (30 min). Students take each mark and give biblical grounds, historical evidence and a present-day example (one: Ephesians 4:4–6, the Mass worldwide; holy: saints; catholic: 1.3 billion on every continent; apostolic: unbroken lines of bishops). Debate: how do wounds to unity challenge the first mark?',
 teach=['One: one Lord, one faith, one Baptism (CCC 813–822). Unity is a gift; divisions among Christians are a wound. The Church works for Christian unity (ecumenism, CCC 820–822).',
        'Holy: because Christ sanctifies her through sacraments and the Spirit (CCC 823–829). The Church has saints in every age and sinners too.',
        'Catholic: “according to the whole”: the fullness of faith and means of salvation, and sent to all people (CCC 830–856). Apostolic: founded on the apostles, teaching their doctrine and governed by their successors (CCC 857–865).',
        'Teach respectfully about other Christians, who share Baptism and many truths (CCC 818–819).'],
 misc=['“If the Church is holy, no one in it can be bad.”', '“Catholic means ‘Roman’ and nothing more.”'],
 mem='“There is one body and one Spirit… one Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God and Father of us all.” (Ephesians 4:4–6)',
 assess='Ask students to name the four marks, explain each and give one sign that it is real today.'),
'8-2-1': dict(
 idea='The first three centuries were years of persecution. Martyrs such as Stephen, Ignatius of Antioch, Polycarp, Perpetua and Felicity bore witness to Christ with their lives. “The blood of the martyrs is the seed of Christians.”',
 hook='Read Acts 7:54–60 and ask what Stephen says as he dies. Note the echo of Jesus’ words from the Cross.',
 activity='“Martyr profiles” (30 min). Groups read short excerpts from the Martyrdom of Polycarp and the Passion of Perpetua and Felicity, then make a profile card: who, when, charge, their words, what they teach us. Present, then discuss modern martyrdom today.',
 teach=['The word “martyr” means “witness” (CCC 2473). Martyrdom is the supreme witness to the truth of faith (CCC 2473–2474). The Church honors martyrs as models and intercessors.',
        'Ignatius of Antioch (c. 107) wrote on the way to Rome, “Let me be food for the wild beasts.” Polycarp (c. 155) refused to deny Christ: “Eighty-six years I have served him.”',
        'Perpetua and Felicity (203) show young women’s courage. Tertullian wrote that “the blood of martyrs is the seed of Christians.”',
        'More Christians died for the faith in the twentieth century than in all the previous centuries combined. Speak with sensitivity and hope.'],
 misc=['“Martyrs wanted to die.”', '“Persecution ended with Rome.”'],
 mem='“Lord Jesus, receive my spirit… Lord, do not hold this sin against them.” (Acts 7:59–60)',
 assess='Ask students to name two martyrs, what they said or did and what we learn from them.'),
'8-2-2': dict(
 idea='In 313 Constantine ended the persecution of Christians. Soon the Church faced heresies, especially about who Christ is. Councils at Nicaea (325), Constantinople (381), Ephesus (431) and Chalcedon (451) defined the faith still professed in the Creed.',
 hook='Ask: “Is Jesus a creature, or is he God?” Explain that the answer wasn’t obvious to everyone until the Church spoke clearly.',
 activity='“Compare the creeds” (30 min). Place the Apostles’ Creed and the Nicene Creed side by side. Students highlight what Nicaea added (“begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father”). Then match each council with the heresy it answered: Arianism, Macedonianism, Nestorianism, Monophysitism.',
 teach=['The Edict of Milan (313) gave Christians freedom of worship. Constantine called the Council of Nicaea (325) to settle Arius’ claim that the Son was a creature. Nicaea taught that the Son is “consubstantial” (homoousios) with the Father (CCC 465, 242).',
        'Constantinople (381) defended the divinity of the Holy Spirit and completed the Nicene Creed. Ephesus (431) called Mary Theotokos. Chalcedon (451) taught two natures in one Person (CCC 466–469).',
        'The Church defines doctrine to protect the faith, not to invent it (CCC 88–90). The councils worked under the Spirit with the Pope’s authority.',
        'Constantine’s patronage helped the Church but also brought entanglement with the state. Be honest about both sides.'],
 misc=['“Constantine invented Christian doctrine at Nicaea.”', '“Councils added new beliefs.”'],
 mem='“We believe in one Lord, Jesus Christ, the only Son of God, … consubstantial with the Father.” (Nicene Creed)',
 assess='Ask students to explain one council, the heresy it answered and the teaching it defined.'),
'8-2-3': dict(
 idea='The Fathers of the Church are early teachers and bishops whose writings shaped doctrine and prayer. Ignatius, Justin, Irenaeus, Athanasius, Basil, Gregory, Chrysostom, Ambrose, Jerome and Augustine are among them.',
 hook='Ask: “Who teaches the person who teaches you?” Read 2 Timothy 2:2 and talk about the chain from Paul to Timothy to others.',
 activity='“Read a Father” (30 min). Provide short excerpts: Athanasius (On the Incarnation 54: “He became man that we might become God”), Augustine (Confessions 1.1: “our heart is restless”), Chrysostom (Homily on Matthew). Students summarize, note the main idea, and decide what is still relevant.',
 teach=['The Fathers were early witnesses to Tradition (CCC 11, 78, 688, 2651). Their agreement is a mark of the Church’s faith (Dei Verbum 8).',
        'Some Fathers are Doctors of the Church. Jerome translated the Vulgate; Augustine wrote the Confessions and City of God; Athanasius defended Nicaea; John Chrysostom (“golden mouth”) preached eloquently.',
        'They wrote Scripture commentaries, creeds and catechetical lectures. Their approach is practical, pastoral and prayerful.',
        'Men and women: also mention women like Monica, Macrina and Perpetua, who shaped them.'],
 misc=['“The Fathers all agreed on everything.”', '“They are only for scholars.”'],
 mem='“O God, you have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.” (St Augustine)',
 assess='Ask students to name three Fathers, one work or idea each, and why they matter.'),
'8-3-1': dict(
 idea='When persecution ended, some men and women withdrew to follow Christ more radically in prayer and work. Anthony in the Egyptian desert, Pachomius and Basil began communities; Benedict wrote a Rule built on “ora et labora” (pray and work). Monks preserved learning and carried the Gospel to new lands.',
 hook='Ask: “What would you do with a day of silence? What would you pray for?” Read Matthew 19:21 about the rich young man.',
 activity='“A Benedictine day” (35 min). Examine the daily horarium of a monastery: Vigils, Lauds, work, lectio, Vespers, Compline. Students create a schedule that balances prayer, work and rest, and try silent lectio for five minutes.',
 teach=['Consecrated life is a gift to the Church, a call to follow the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience (CCC 914–933). Monastic life began with Anthony of Egypt (c. 270).',
        'Benedict’s Rule (c. 530) stresses stability, obedience, hospitality and a balanced day: “Let nothing be preferred to the work of God.” Benedictines copied manuscripts and preserved classical texts.',
        'Missionaries such as Patrick (Ireland), Augustine of Canterbury (England), Boniface (Germany) and Cyril and Methodius (Slavs) spread the Gospel.',
        'Monastic communities still welcome guests. Invite students to consider visiting one.'],
 misc=['“Monks run away from the world.”', '“The Middle Ages were all darkness.”'],
 mem='“If you would be perfect, go, sell what you possess and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me.” (Matthew 19:21)',
 assess='Ask students to explain “ora et labora” and how monks preserved the faith.'),
'8-3-2': dict(
 idea='The Middle Ages produced great cathedrals, universities and saints. Faith shaped art, music and learning. Thomas Aquinas, Francis of Assisi, Dominic and Catherine of Siena show different ways to holiness.',
 hook='Show a photo of Chartres or Notre-Dame’s interior. Ask: “What do you notice? Why do the windows soar up?”',
 activity='“Cathedral tour” (30 min). Take a virtual tour of a Gothic cathedral. Students identify the cross-shaped plan, rose window, flying buttress, stained glass stories. Then read Psalm 27:4 and write a reflection: “What do I desire from the house of the Lord?”',
 teach=['Faith inspired beauty: the Church’s tradition holds that beauty leads to God (CCC 2500–2503). Cathedrals were “Bibles in stone and glass” for a largely illiterate people.',
        'Universities (Paris, Bologna, Oxford) grew out of cathedral schools. Aquinas’ Summa Theologiae joined faith and reason (CCC 31–35, 159).',
        'Francis of Assisi embraced poverty and creation; Dominic founded the Order of Preachers; Catherine of Siena counseled popes. Women such as Hildegard and Clare shaped the age.',
        'Be honest about the flaws (wars, corruption). The era was complex, not purely golden.'],
 misc=['“Medieval people did not think; they only prayed.”', '“Cathedrals were built to show off wealth.”'],
 mem='“One thing have I asked of the Lord, that will I seek after: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life.” (Psalm 27:4)',
 assess='Ask students to name three medieval achievements and what they show about faith and culture.'),
'8-3-3': dict(
 idea='In 1054 the Churches of East and West formally divided, after centuries of growing apart. The causes included authority (the role of the Pope), the Filioque, language, culture and politics. Jesus’ prayer “that they may all be one” makes this a wound we pray to heal.',
 hook='Read John 17:20–23 and ask what Jesus prays for. Then show a map of the Roman Empire divided and ask how distance might cause misunderstanding.',
 activity='“Map the division” (30 min). Mark Rome and Constantinople, the Latin- and Greek-speaking worlds, the 1054 excommunications (Cardinal Humbert and Patriarch Michael Cerularius). List causes. Then read the 1965 lifting of excommunications (Paul VI and Athenagoras).',
 teach=['The Eastern Orthodox and Catholic Churches share apostolic succession, sacraments and the Creed, but differ on papal primacy and jurisdiction, the Filioque and other points (CCC 817–819, 838).',
        'The sack of Constantinople by Crusaders in 1204 deepened the rift. Attempts at reunion at Lyon (1274) and Florence (1439) did not last.',
        'Today the Church seeks unity: Pope John Paul II said the Church must “breathe with both lungs.” Dialogue continues (CCC 820–822).',
        'The 23 Eastern Catholic Churches are in full communion with Rome while keeping their own traditions.'],
 misc=['“The East left the Church over one word.”', '“Orthodox are not Christian.”'],
 mem='“That they may all be one, even as you, Father, are in me, and I in you.” (John 17:21)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the main causes of the schism and one step toward healing.'),
'8-4-1': dict(
 idea='The Reformation began in 1517 when Martin Luther challenged the sale of indulgences. It grew into a wider break from Rome about authority, Scripture, justification and the sacraments, giving rise to Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican traditions. It left lasting wounds in Christian unity.',
 hook='Ask: “When is it right to speak out about a problem in an institution you love? What can go wrong?” Introduce Luther as a case.',
 activity='“Key issues chart” (30 min). Students build a chart with columns: issue, Reformers’ position, Catholic position (authority: sola scriptura vs. Scripture and Tradition; justification: faith alone vs. faith working through love; sacraments: two vs. seven; the Mass; the papacy). Include real abuses: indulgence sellers, simony, clerical corruption.',
 teach=['The abuses were real. Many Catholics, including saints, asked for reform. Luther’s Ninety-five Theses (1517) challenged indulgence preaching (CCC 1471–1479). The break widened with the Diet of Worms (1521).',
        'Reformers stressed Scripture alone, faith alone, grace alone. Catholics affirm Scripture, Tradition and the Magisterium, and justification by grace through faith that works in love (CCC 80–82, 1987–1995).',
        'Henry VIII’s break with Rome (1534) was political. Calvin’s Geneva developed Reformed theology. Wars of religion followed.',
        'Since Vatican II the Church recognizes our separated brothers and sisters as Christians and seeks unity (CCC 817–819).'],
 misc=['“Luther simply wanted to leave the Church.”', '“All Reformers agreed.”'],
 mem='“I appeal to you, brethren… that there be no dissensions among you, but that you be united in the same mind.” (1 Corinthians 1:10)',
 assess='Ask students to explain three issues in the Reformation and how Catholics responded.'),
'8-4-2': dict(
 idea='The Council of Trent (1545–1563) responded to the Reformation by clarifying doctrine and reforming the Church. It defined the canon, the sacraments, justification and the Mass, and set up seminaries, a catechism and stricter discipline.',
 hook='Ask: “If your school needed to fix problems and explain its rules, what would you do?” Compare to Trent’s doctrine and reform.',
 activity='“Trent summary” (30 min). Students read a summary of Trent’s decrees and sort them into doctrine and reform. Then compare the Roman Catechism (1566) to today’s Catechism of the Catholic Church. Read 2 Timothy 4:1–5.',
 teach=['Trent affirmed seven sacraments, transubstantiation, the Mass as sacrifice and the canon of 73 books (CCC 120, 1376, 1129).',
        'It taught justification by grace, with cooperation of the free will (CCC 2008–2010). It rejected the claim that grace is only external.',
        'Reforms: seminaries for priests, bishops to reside in their dioceses, the Roman Catechism, a standard missal. These produced saints like Charles Borromeo.',
        'The Counter-Reformation also brought Baroque art and new orders (Jesuits, Capuchins) and renewed missions.'],
 misc=['“Trent invented new doctrines.”', '“Trent was only a reaction against Protestants.”'],
 mem='“Preach the word; be ready in season and out of season.” (2 Timothy 4:2)',
 assess='Ask students to name two doctrinal and two disciplinary results of Trent.'),
'8-4-3': dict(
 idea='New religious orders and missionaries carried the Gospel across the world. Ignatius of Loyola founded the Jesuits; Francis Xavier traveled to India and Japan; Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross renewed the Carmelites; Francis de Sales taught holiness for everyone.',
 hook='Show a world map with lines to Asia, Africa and the Americas. Ask: “What would it take to go there in 1550?”',
 activity='“Biography presentations” (35 min). Each student presents a 2-minute biography: Ignatius, Francis Xavier, Teresa of Ávila, John of the Cross, Francis de Sales, Rose of Lima, Isaac Jogues. Include a quote and a lesson for today. Link to Matthew 28:19.',
 teach=['The Church is missionary by nature (CCC 849–856). The age of exploration brought both heroic missionaries and entanglement with colonialism; some defended native peoples (Bartolomé de las Casas).',
        'Ignatius’ Spiritual Exercises teach discernment. Teresa and John teach contemplative prayer (CCC 2709–2719). Francis de Sales taught the “devout life” for ordinary people (CCC 2013).',
        'Missionary martyrs include the North American Martyrs, Japanese martyrs and many in China and Africa. The Church grows by witness.',
        'Name some saints from your own culture to show how the Gospel takes root in each people.'],
 misc=['“Missionaries were only agents of empire.”', '“Only priests can be missionaries.”'],
 mem='“Go therefore and make disciples of all nations.” (Matthew 28:19)',
 assess='Ask students to present a saint’s contribution and what it teaches today.'),
'8-5-1': dict(
 idea='The Second Vatican Council (1962–1965), called by John XXIII, renewed the Church’s life and mission. It issued sixteen documents, including four constitutions: on the Church (Lumen Gentium), Divine Revelation (Dei Verbum), the Liturgy (Sacrosanctum Concilium) and the Church in the modern world (Gaudium et Spes).',
 hook='Say: “Open the windows,” which is what John XXIII said about the Council. Ask what that image suggests.',
 activity='“Four constitutions” (35 min). Groups take one constitution, read a short excerpt and list its main ideas and a practical result (Mass in the vernacular, a call to holiness for all, Scripture’s place, dialogue with the world). Create a one-page summary and present.',
 teach=['The Council did not change doctrine but deepened and renewed its expression (CCC 10–11). Lumen Gentium: the Church as mystery and People of God, the universal call to holiness (CCC 770–776, 2013).',
        'Dei Verbum: Scripture and Tradition; Sacrosanctum Concilium: full, conscious, active participation in the liturgy (CCC 1071–1075). Gaudium et Spes: the Church’s joy and hope, grief and anguish for all people.',
        'Other documents: on ecumenism (Unitatis Redintegratio), non-Christian religions (Nostra Aetate), religious freedom (Dignitatis Humanae).',
        'Be honest that interpretations differ, and encourage reading the texts themselves.'],
 misc=['“Vatican II abolished the old Mass or doctrine.”', '“The Council changed Church teaching on everything.”'],
 mem='“I came that they may have life and have it abundantly.” (John 10:10)',
 assess='Ask students to name the four constitutions and one lasting fruit of each.'),
'8-5-2': dict(
 idea='Catholic social teaching applies the Gospel to society. Its seven themes — dignity of the person, family and community, rights and responsibilities, option for the poor, dignity of work, solidarity, care for creation — guide how we treat people and build the common good.',
 hook='Read Luke 4:18–19. Ask: “What does ‘good news to the poor’ mean in practice?”',
 activity='“Match themes to examples” (30 min). Cards with themes and cases (a living wage, a refugee family, a polluted river, a bullied classmate, a school food drive). Students match, explain and propose a step they can take.',
 teach=['The tradition begins with Leo XIII’s Rerum Novarum (1891) on the rights of workers and continues through Pius XI, John XXIII, Paul VI, John Paul II, Benedict XVI and Francis (CCC 2419–2425).',
        'Principles: the dignity of every person (CCC 1700–1709), the common good (CCC 1905–1912), subsidiarity (CCC 1883–1885), solidarity (CCC 1939–1942), preferential love for the poor (CCC 2443–2449).',
        'The Church teaches that work is for the person, and that creation is a gift to be shared and protected (CCC 2426–2436, 2415–2418).',
        'Social teaching is not partisan. It challenges every ideology.'],
 misc=['“Social teaching is just politics.”', '“Charity is enough; justice is not the Church’s business.”'],
 mem='“The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me to proclaim good news to the poor.” (Luke 4:18)',
 assess='Ask students to name four principles and apply two to a case.'),
'8-5-3': dict(
 idea='The Catholic Church includes 24 self-governing Churches in full communion with the Pope: the Latin Church and 23 Eastern Catholic Churches. They share one faith and sacraments but have their own liturgies, laws and traditions. Revelation 7:9 pictures every nation worshiping together.',
 hook='Display the Sign of the Cross in different languages or rites. Ask: “Is it the same prayer?” Explore unity in diversity.',
 activity='“Churches on a map” (30 min). Students mark the Latin Church and several Eastern Catholic Churches (Maronite, Melkite, Ukrainian Greek Catholic, Syro-Malabar, Chaldean, Coptic, Armenian) on a world map. Add one distinctive feature each (liturgical language, icons, married clergy, chrismation of infants).',
 teach=['The Church is catholic in her unity and diversity (CCC 830–838). The Eastern Catholic Churches use the Byzantine, Alexandrian, Antiochene, Armenian and Chaldean rites (CCC 1200–1206).',
        'Many Eastern Catholic Churches ordain married men to the priesthood; infants receive Baptism, Chrismation and Communion together (CCC 1290, 1233).',
        'Eastern Orthodox Churches are not in full communion but share much. Learning from them enriches our faith.',
        'Visit an Eastern Catholic parish if possible.'],
 misc=['“Catholic means Roman Catholic only.”', '“Eastern Catholics are Orthodox who converted.”'],
 mem='“After this I looked, and behold, a great multitude that no one could number, from every nation, from all tribes and peoples and languages.” (Revelation 7:9)',
 assess='Ask students to name five Churches, their rite and one distinctive practice.'),
'8-6-1': dict(
 idea='The Holy Spirit is the soul of the Church. He sanctifies her, teaches her all truth, gives her gifts and charisms, and unites her to Christ. Jesus promised the Spirit as Advocate and Spirit of truth.',
 hook='Read John 14:16–17. Ask: “What does ‘Advocate’ mean?” (someone called alongside to help). The Spirit never leaves the Church.',
 activity='“Study Pentecost and beyond” (30 min). Read Acts 2 and John 14–16. Students chart the Spirit’s work: teaches, recalls Jesus’ words, convicts of sin, guides to truth, empowers witnesses. Add examples from Church history (councils, saints) and personal life.',
 teach=['The Spirit is “the soul of the Church” (CCC 797–798). He builds up the Body through the sacraments, the Word, charisms and ministries (CCC 799–801).',
        'Jesus promised, “He will teach you all things and bring to your remembrance all that I have said to you” (John 14:26). The Spirit guides the Church’s Magisterium (CCC 91–93).',
        'Charisms are special graces for the good of the Church (1 Corinthians 12; CCC 799–801). They must be discerned and ordered by pastors.',
        'Teach epiclesis: the Church calls on the Spirit at every sacrament (CCC 1105–1109).'],
 misc=['“The Spirit works only in emotional experiences.”', '“Only a few are given the Spirit.”'],
 mem='“The Advocate, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name, he will teach you all things.” (John 14:26)',
 assess='Ask students to describe three ways the Spirit acts in the Church.'),
'8-6-2': dict(
 idea='Isaiah names seven gifts of the Spirit (wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord). Paul lists the fruits (love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, generosity, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control and others). The gifts are given, the fruits ripen.',
 hook='Show a fruit tree. Ask: “How do you know what kind of tree it is?” By its fruit. Read Galatians 5:22–23.',
 activity='“Memorize and apply” (30 min). Students memorize the seven gifts and the fruits. Then play a matching game: scenarios (a hard decision, bullying, a test) with the gift that helps. Pray the Veni Creator or Come Holy Spirit.',
 teach=['The seven gifts (Isaiah 11:1–3) make us docile to the Spirit’s promptings (CCC 1830–1831). Wisdom and understanding perfect faith, counsel and fortitude guide action, knowledge helps us see things as God does, piety deepens love and reverence, fear of the Lord is wonder and awe.',
        'The fruits of the Spirit are “the first fruits of eternal glory” (CCC 1832): charity, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, generosity, gentleness, faithfulness, modesty, self-control, chastity.',
        'Gifts are received at Baptism and strengthened in Confirmation. Fruits show the Spirit at work.',
        'Distinguish gifts from charisms and from virtues.'],
 misc=['“Fear of the Lord means being terrified.”', '“Gifts are for the holy; fruits are optional.”'],
 mem='“The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.” (Galatians 5:22–23)',
 assess='Ask students to recite the seven gifts and give an example of a fruit in daily life.'),
'8-6-3': dict(
 idea='Confirmation completes baptismal grace. The bishop anoints the forehead with chrism and says, “Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.” We are strengthened, bound more closely to the Church, and sent to bear witness.',
 hook='Ask: “What do you need to be ready for a big responsibility?” (training, support, strength). Confirmation is God’s strengthening.',
 activity='“Prepare the rite” (35 min). Walk through the rite: presentation of candidates, homily, renewal of baptismal promises, laying on of hands, anointing with chrism, general intercessions. Students write what they will say in their own words, and prepare a service project.',
 teach=['Confirmation, with Baptism and the Eucharist, completes initiation (CCC 1285). It imprints an indelible spiritual mark and increases the gifts of the Spirit (CCC 1302–1305).',
        'The essential rite is anointing with chrism, together with the words (CCC 1300). In the Latin Church, the bishop is the ordinary minister (CCC 1312–1314).',
        'Effects: deeper rootedness in divine sonship, closer union with Christ, increase of the Spirit’s gifts, a more perfect bond with the Church, strength to witness (CCC 1303).',
        'Requirements: to be baptized, in a state of grace, with proper instruction. A sponsor accompanies the candidate (CCC 1307–1311).'],
 misc=['“Confirmation is when I choose to be Catholic.”', '“I can skip Confirmation and still be fully initiated.”'],
 mem='Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit. (Rite of Confirmation)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the rite, its effects and what it asks of them.'),
'8-7-1': dict(
 idea='Freedom is not doing whatever we want but the power to choose the good. Conscience is the inner judgment that helps us see what is right, and it must be formed. True freedom grows as we practice virtue.',
 hook='Present two images: a train on tracks and a train off the tracks. “Which is free to go where it was made to go?” Freedom is for something.',
 activity='“Case study debate” (35 min). Present cases (peer pressure to cheat, a rumor, a risky dare, honesty about a mistake). Students apply the steps of conscience: pray, learn what the Church teaches, seek advice, weigh consequences, choose. Discuss which choices lead to freedom.',
 teach=['Freedom is “the power, rooted in reason and will, to act or not to act, to do this or that” (CCC 1731). It grows to perfection when directed to God (CCC 1731, 1744). “For freedom Christ has set us free” (Galatians 5:1).',
        'The human act is judged by its object, intention and circumstances (CCC 1750–1756). A good intention does not make a bad act good.',
        'Conscience must be formed; one is obliged to follow a certain conscience (CCC 1776–1785). Ignorance may lessen responsibility, but we should seek the truth.',
        'Freedom is limited by habits, fear and social pressure, but never destroyed (CCC 1735–1748).'],
 misc=['“Freedom is doing what I want.”', '“If I feel good about it, my conscience is clear.”'],
 mem='“For freedom Christ has set us free; stand firm therefore.” (Galatians 5:1)',
 assess='Ask students to explain true freedom and the steps of forming a conscience with an example.'),
'8-7-2': dict(
 idea='Virtue builds the good; sin harms. Virtues are habits that make doing good easier. The capital sins — pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony and sloth — are root vices. The remedy is grace and daily practice.',
 hook='Ask: “What does an athlete do to become strong?” Daily practice. The same is true for virtues; vice is a habit too.',
 activity='“Growth plan” (30 min). Each student identifies one virtue to grow and the opposing vice, plans three daily practices, a support (friend, prayer, sacrament) and a way to track progress. Read Romans 7:15–25 as a sign that struggle is normal.',
 teach=['Virtue is a firm disposition to do good (CCC 1803). Human virtues are acquired; theological virtues are gifts (CCC 1804–1829).',
        'Sin is an offense against God, reason and truth (CCC 1849). It creates a tendency to more sin (vice). The capital sins give rise to others (CCC 1866).',
        'Romans 7: “I do not do what I want, but I do the very thing I hate.” Paul shows both struggle and hope: “Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ.”',
        'Address both pride and scrupulosity. God’s grace strengthens; examine yourself without despair.'],
 misc=['“I can overcome all sin by willpower.”', '“Everyone sins, so it doesn’t matter.”'],
 mem='“Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ our Lord!” (Romans 7:25)',
 assess='Ask students to name the seven capital sins with their opposing virtues.'),
'8-7-3': dict(
 idea='Every human life is sacred from conception to natural death because each person is made in God’s image. The fifth commandment forbids murder, abortion, euthanasia and suicide, and calls us to protect life and care for the vulnerable.',
 hook='Read Psalm 139:13–16 aloud. Ask: “What does this say about how God sees each of us before anyone else does?”',
 activity='“Service project” (35 min). Plan a project that honors life: visit a nursing home, help a pro-life pregnancy center, make cards for the sick, write to a lonely person. Prepare respectfully and discuss how to speak with charity about hard topics.',
 teach=['Human life is sacred (CCC 2258). Direct and intentional killing of an innocent person is gravely wrong (CCC 2268–2269). Abortion is a grave offense (CCC 2270–2275).',
        'Euthanasia and assisted suicide are morally unacceptable; legitimate refusal of burdensome treatment is allowed (CCC 2276–2279). Suicide is contrary to love of self and God, though the Church prays for those who die this way (CCC 2280–2283).',
        'The Church also protects life by defending the poor, the immigrant, the prisoner, and by promoting peace (CCC 2302–2317). The death penalty is now judged inadmissible (CCC 2267).',
        'Offer compassion to women facing difficult pregnancies and to those who have suffered abortion or loss; Project Rachel offers healing.'],
 misc=['“The Church cares about life only before birth.”', '“All end-of-life care must be continued.”'],
 mem='“For you formed my inward parts; you knit me together in my mother’s womb.” (Psalm 139:13)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the dignity of life and two ways to protect it.'),
'8-8-1': dict(
 idea='Prayer takes three main expressions: vocal prayer with words, meditation with thought, and contemplation, a silent gaze of love. All three have a place in a mature life of prayer.',
 hook='Say the Jesus Prayer slowly for one minute in silence. Ask what they noticed. Introduce the three expressions.',
 activity='“Try three forms” (35 min). Stations: vocal (pray the Our Father slowly), meditation (read Matthew 6:25–34 and imagine the scene), contemplation (sit with the Jesus Prayer: “Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me”). Students write a brief reflection after each.',
 teach=['Vocal prayer unites body and spirit; Jesus taught us the Our Father (CCC 2700–2704). Meditation seeks to understand the why and how of the Christian life (CCC 2705–2708).',
        'Contemplative prayer is “a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus… a silent love” (CCC 2709–2719). It is a gift, not a technique.',
        'Obstacles: distraction, dryness, acedia (CCC 2729–2733). Remedies: persevering, humility, simple trust.',
        'Encourage a daily rhythm: morning offering, mealtime, a quiet ten minutes, examen at night.'],
 misc=['“If my mind wanders, I’m failing.”', '“Meditation means emptying my mind.”'],
 mem='“Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner.” (The Jesus Prayer)',
 assess='Ask students to describe each expression of prayer and which they will practice this week.'),
'8-8-2': dict(
 idea='The Eucharist feeds us as the bread of life, and Eucharistic adoration extends the Mass by prayer before the Blessed Sacrament. Jesus said, “Whoever eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life.”',
 hook='Ask: “What does food do?” (feeds, strengthens, shares). Read John 6:35 and 6:51 and note what Jesus says about himself as food.',
 activity='“Visit the Blessed Sacrament” (30 min). Take students to church for a silent visit. Teach how to genuflect, kneel and pray: adoration, thanksgiving, sorrow, petition (ACTS). Afterwards, write a prayer and share what struck them.',
 teach=['The Eucharist is the Body and Blood, Soul and Divinity of Christ (CCC 1374). In John 6 Jesus says his flesh is “true food” (John 6:55) and many disciples left rather than accept it.',
        'Eucharistic worship outside Mass is a prolongation of Mass (CCC 1378–1381). The tabernacle, Benediction and Forty Hours devotion honor Christ present.',
        'The fruits of Communion: union with Christ, forgiveness of venial sin, protection from mortal sin, unity of the Church (CCC 1391–1397).',
        'Teach the Church’s norms of preparation and reverence (CCC 1385–1389).'],
 misc=['“Adoration is staring at bread.”', '“Communion is optional if I go to Mass.”'],
 mem='“I am the living bread that came down from heaven; if anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever.” (John 6:51)',
 assess='Ask students to explain Real Presence and how adoration relates to Mass.'),
'8-8-3': dict(
 idea='Every baptized Catholic shares in the Church’s mission to evangelize. We witness by how we live and by what we say, with respect and joy. Evangelization begins with a personal encounter with Jesus.',
 hook='Ask: “If someone asked why you believe, what would you say in 30 seconds?” Then read Matthew 28:19–20 and 1 Peter 3:15.',
 activity='“Witness project” (35 min). Students write a 30-second testimony (“how God has been present to me”), then plan a witness project: a service day, inviting a friend to Mass, a social media post, or a prayer group. Practice with partners.',
 teach=['Evangelization means proclaiming the Gospel to bring people to Christ (CCC 849–856, 863). All the baptized share in this mission through their baptismal priesthood (CCC 900, 905).',
        'The Church teaches that mission respects freedom (CCC 160, 2104–2109). Pope Francis stresses that the Church grows by attraction, not proclaiming at others.',
        'Witness of life, loving service and clear explanation work together. “Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence” (1 Peter 3:15).',
        'Mention the new evangelization, called for by John Paul II.'],
 misc=['“Evangelization is only for missionaries.”', '“Evangelization means arguing people into the Church.”'],
 mem='“Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you.” (1 Peter 3:15)',
 assess='Ask students to state their testimony and one concrete witness step.'),
}
